cff-version: 1.2.0
title: Meridian Financial Constitution Corpus
message: >-
  If you use this corpus, please cite it and the Digital Judiciary programme
  papers it instantiates.
type: dataset
version: 2.4.0
license: CC-BY-4.0
license-url: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
authors:
  - family-names: Kadambi
    given-names: Arun
  - family-names: Kuppuswamy
    given-names: Venkat
    affiliation: >-
      D'Amore-McKim School of Business, Northeastern University
abstract: >-
  An open 6,000-case conformance corpus for governing software change in a
  regulated financial institution under a Digital Judiciary. A constitution of
  116 provisions (24 absolute) across financial-crime screening and BSA/AML,
  payment card data, secrets and cryptography, software supply chain, change
  control, ledger integrity and idempotency, numeric precision, operational
  resilience and ICT third-party risk, data privacy and residency, credit
  decisioning and fair lending, AI governance, agent-authored change,
  consumer protection, audit evidence, and governance of the judiciary itself.
  It is a superset of the released Meridian Financial pack: its thirty-seven
  provisions, each citing a real regulatory instrument, are reproduced with
  text and absolute flags unchanged. Seven findings against them — including
  that SR 11-7 was superseded on 17 April 2026, that two CFPB circulars were
  withdrawn, that the Reg B effects test was removed effective 21 July 2026,
  and that one absolute provision is drafted stricter than the rule it cites —
  are filed as draft amendments rather than applied. Ground truth is computed
  from an executed trace. Ships a reference adjudication engine, 26 conformance
  checks with a strengthened pressure-invariance check, a mutation matrix, and
  a five-pack profile comparison.
keywords:
  - compliance
  - precedent
  - policy-as-code
  - fintech
  - financial regulation
  - model risk management
  - fair lending
  - conformance testing
references:
  - type: article
    title: >-
      When Retrieval Binds: Appraisal, Authenticity and Disposition in
      Automated Compliance Decisions
    authors:
